          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/landscape+of+string+theory+vacua"} vacuum
            is encoded by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            2-dimensional
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SCFT"} SCFT
            %em heterotic string theory
            that SCFT is assumed to be the sum of a supersymmetric chiral piece and a non-supersymmetric piece (therefore “heterotic”).
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#compactifications Compactifications
          %p
            An effective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            induced from a given heterotic 2d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CFT"} CFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma+model"} sigma model
            that has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi M
                    %mn 4
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mn 6
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M^4 \times Y^6
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi M
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M^4
            the 4-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+space"} Minkowski space
            that is experimentally observed locally (say on the scale of a particle accelerator) has
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N= 1
            global
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            precisely if the remaining 6-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mn 6
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^6
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+manifold"} Calabi-Yau manifold
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#ReferencesNEqOne"} references below
          %p
            Since global
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N=1
            supersymmetry for a long time has been considered a promising phenomenological model in high energy physics, this fact has induced a lot of interest in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string+theory+on+CY3-manifolds"} heterotic string theory on CY3-manifolds
          %h3#enhanced_supersymmetry Enhanced supersymmetry
          %p
            A priori the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+SCFT"} 2d SCFT
            describing the quantum heterotic string has
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N=(1,0)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective field theory
            has
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N=1
            supersymmetry does the worldsheet theory enhance to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N=(2,0)
            supersymmetry. See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+%282%2C0%29-superconformal+QFT"} 2d (2,0)-superconformal QFT
            and at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+manifolds+and+supersymmetry"} Calabi-Yau manifolds and supersymmetry
            for more on this.
          %h3#GaugeGroups Gauge groups
          %p
            Precisely two (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism+classes"} isomorphism classes
            of)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+groups"} gauge groups
            are consistent (give
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomaly+cancellation"} quantum anomaly cancellation
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                while preserving
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+product+group"} direct product group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 8
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 8
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_8 \times E_8
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+Lie+group"} exceptional Lie group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E8"} E8
            with itself, the other is in fact the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-spin+group"} semi-spin group
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SemiSpin%2832%29"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi SemiSpin
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 32
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SemiSpin(32)
          %p
            If the supersymmetry requirement is dropped, then there is a third option, which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} locally
            looks like the product of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+orthogonal+group"} special orthogonal group
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SO
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 16
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %mi SO
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 16
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SO(16)\times SO(16)
            %a{:href => "#AGMV86het"} Alvarez-Gaumé, Ginsparg, Moore, &amp; Vafa 1986
            and by
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#DH86het"} Dixon &amp; Harvey 1986
            %a{:href => "#SW87anom"} Schellekens &amp; Warner 1987
            that this theory could be regarded as some sort of “difference” (a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondence"} correspondence
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+in+string+theory"} duality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 8
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 8
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_8\times E_8
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SemiSpin%2832%29"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mtext SemiSpin
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 32
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{SemiSpin}(32)
            theories, by looking at how the relevant representations of these two theories assemble into those appearing in the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SO
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 16
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi SO
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 16
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SO(16)\times SO(16)
            theory.
          %p
            The
            %em global
            character of the gauge group is not
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SO
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 16
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %mi SO
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 16
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SO(16)\times SO(16)
            %a{:href => "#McIn99spin"} McInnes 1999
            proposed that the actual gauge group is a quotient of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+group"} spin groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtext Spin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 16
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mtext Spin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 16
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{Spin}(16) \times \text{Spin}(16)
            by a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            (not corresponding to
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mtext SemiSpin
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 16
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %mtext SemiSpin
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 16
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{SemiSpin}(16)\times \text{SemiSpin}(16)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtext Spin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 16
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mtext Spin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 16
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{Spin} (16) \times \text{Spin}(16)/ \mathbb{Z}_2
            as a subgroup of both
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 8
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 8
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_8\times E_8
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mtext SemiSpin
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 32
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{SemiSpin}(32)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtext Spin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 16
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mtext Spin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 16
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{Spin} (16) \times \text{Spin} (16)/\mathbb{Z}_2
            theory as the realization of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-duality"} T-duality
            between the supersymmetric heterotic strings.
          %h3#Dualities Dualities
          %p
            Some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualities+in+string+theory"} dualities in string theory
            involving the heterotic string:
          %h4#duality_with_type_i_string_theory Duality with type I string theory
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+between+heterotic+and+type+I+string+theory"} duality between heterotic and type I string theory
          %h4#duality_with_type_ii_string_theory Duality with type II string theory
          %p
            See
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+between+heterotic+and+type+II+string+theory"} duality between heterotic and type II string theory
          %h4#duality_with_mtheory Duality with M-theory
          %p
            See
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+between+heterotic+string+theory+and+M-theory"} duality between heterotic string theory and M-theory
          %h4#duality_with_ftheory Duality with F-theory
          %p
